WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.670
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:09.740
When looking for illustrations for a presentation,
most of the time, I quickly find an appropriate

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:15.459
photo on the internet, however, many of these
photos are really low resolution.

00:00:15.459 --> 00:00:21.640
This often creates a weird situation where
I have think, okay, do I use the splotchier,

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:27.779
lower-resolution image that gets the point
across, or take a high-resolution, crisp image

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:29.490
that is less educational?

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:34.140
In case you are wondering, I encounter this
problem for almost every single video I make

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:35.320
for this channel.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:40.629
As you can surely tell, I am waiting for the
day when super resolution becomes mainstream.

00:00:40.629 --> 00:00:45.300
Super resolution means that we have a low
resolution image that lacks details, and we

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:51.800
feed it to a computer program, which hallucinates
all the details onto it, creating a crisp,

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.280
high-resolution image.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:58.429
This way, I could take my highly relevant,
but blurry image, improve it, and use it in

00:00:58.429 --> 00:00:59.739
my videos.

00:00:59.739 --> 00:01:04.980
As adding details to images clearly requires
a deep understanding of what is shown in these

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:10.259
images, our seasoned Fellow Scholars immediately
know that learning-based algorithms will be

00:01:10.259 --> 00:01:12.040
ideal for this task.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:16.280
While we are looking at some amazing results
with this new technique, let's talk about

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.369
the two key differences that this method introduces:

00:01:19.369 --> 00:01:24.659
One, it takes a fully progressive approach,
which means that we don't immediately produce

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:31.170
the highest resolution output we are looking
for, but slowly leapfrog our way through intermediate

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:36.420
steps, each of which is only slightly higher
resolution than the input.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:41.380
This means that the final output is produced
over several steps, where each problem is

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.560
only a tiny bit harder than the previous one.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.439
This is often referred to as curriculum learning
and it not only increases the quality of the

00:01:49.439 --> 00:01:56.829
solution, but is also easier to train as solving
each intermediate step is only a little harder

00:01:56.829 --> 00:01:57.950
than the previous one.

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:03.909
It is a bit like how students learn in school:
first, the students are shown some easy introductory

00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:09.570
tasks to get a grasp of a problem, and slowly
work their way towards mastering a field by

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:13.170
solving problems that gradually increase in
difficulty.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:19.810
Two, now, we can start playing with the thought
of using a generative adversarial network.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:24.720
We talk a lot about this architecture in this
series, at this time I will only note that

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.880
training these is fraught with difficulties,
so every bit of help we can get is more than

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:35.420
welcome, so the role of curriculum learning
is to help easing this process.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:40.471
Note that this research field is well-explored,
and has a remarkable number of papers, so

00:02:40.471 --> 00:02:44.180
I was expecting a lot of comparisons against
competing techniques.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:49.780
And when looking at the paper and the supplementary
materials, boy, did I get it!

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:54.490
Make sure to have a look at the paper, it
contains a very exhaustive validation section,

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:59.490
which reveals that if we measure the error
of the solution in terms of human perception,

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:05.460
it is only slightly lower quality than the
best technique, however, this one is five

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:10.940
times quicker, offering a really nice balance
between quality and performance.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:15.180
So, what about the actual numbers for the
execution time?

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:20.480
For instance, upsampling an image to increase
its resolution to twice its original size

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:26.970
takes less than a second, and we can go to
up to even eight times the original resolution,

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:30.040
which also only takes four and a half seconds.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:35.840
The quality and the execution times indicate
that we are again, one step closer to mainstream

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.460
super resolution.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:38.930
What a time to be alive!

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:41.730
The source code of this project is also available.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:44.610
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

